# RME invoice correction — zero-original guard (rule 201)

Evidence: `docs/sprints/fix-rme-invoice-correction-zero-original-guard-1.md`.
Register: `docs/decisions/rme-billing-correction-owner-decisions.md` (D28 interim
guard; D21g and D22 remain PENDING). Extends rules 190, 195, 196, 197, 198, 200.
**Supersedes in part:** rule 196 §23 (the Rp0 ban is now ENFORCED, not procedural)
and rule 197 §14 (an unusable account is now refused by the service's own actor
check before the identity allowlist is asked) — see §L.

## A. Root cause (verified in source on `b76b0ed2`, before this sprint)
1. `prePaymentIneligibility()` refused a NEGATIVE current total, never a Rp0 one.
2. The proposal builder refuses a NEW Rp0 line and a Rp0 RESULTING total (so a
   correction can never ZERO anything), but an UNCHANGED Rp0 line may be CHANGED to
   a positive price. Rp0 original → positive revised passed every check.
3. Production held exactly that case — SPN4 invoice #36, UNPAID, Rp0 — guarded only
   by procedure (rule 196 §8/§23). The pilot-2 verification recorded it as LOW-2.

## B. What "the original total" is (the authority)
4. **Initial issued total = v0.** Never a request field (`original_grand_total`,
   `grand_total`, `original_total`), never a rendered amount, never the object a
   caller passes in. The service reads the invoice row it holds FOR UPDATE.
5. `correction_version == 0` → the invoice was never corrected; the LIVE row is v0.
6. `correction_version > 0` → v0 is the frozen `ORIGINAL` document version
   (`version_number = 0`, `version_kind = ORIGINAL`) written by the first
   application; its `snapshot.totals.grand_total_cents` is the authority
   (`RmeInvoiceCorrectionRepositoryInterface::originalDocumentVersion()`).
7. `correction_version > 0` with no frozen v0, a v0 whose snapshot no longer hashes
   to its recorded `snapshot_sha256` (`integrityHolds()` false — an out-of-band
   edit), or a snapshot without an integer total →
   `CORRECTION_ORIGINAL_TOTAL_UNVERIFIABLE` — fail closed, never guess.
8. Distinct quantities, never collapsed: initial issued total (v0), current
   version total, requested revised total (server-computed by the builder), already
   applied corrections (vN document versions), paid amount, remaining balance.

## C. The guard — a TEMPORARY FAIL-CLOSED boundary
9. `ZERO_ORIGINAL_CORRECTION_GUARD = TEMPORARY_FAIL_CLOSED`. Refused with
   `[CORRECTION_ORIGINAL_TOTAL_ZERO]` when v0 is Rp0, OR when the CURRENT total is
   Rp0 (the same outcome reached from a later version). A negative v0 reuses
   `CORRECTION_INVOICE_TOTAL_NEGATIVE`.
10. It refuses regardless of the revised total: positive, zero, zero-by-discount,
    over-discounted. It answers BEFORE the proposal builder is consulted.
11. **D21g = PENDING. D22 = PENDING.** This guard decides neither. It is NOT a
    permanent Rp0 business policy, NOT a new pricing rule, NOT a discount policy.
    Never mark D21g or D22 approved on the strength of this rule; never relax the
    guard without a recorded owner decision on D21g/D22 AND a reviewed code change.
12. The guard lives in ONE place (`zeroOriginalIneligibility()` +
    `originalTotalCents()` in `RmeInvoiceCorrectionService`) and is consulted by
    submit (through `prePaymentIneligibility()`, also feeding the cashier panel) and
    by approve/apply (`assertNotZeroOriginal()`). Never duplicate it in a
    controller, FormRequest, view or policy — those are courtesies.
13. **Issuance is untouched.** A Rp0 invoice can still be issued (consultation,
    Kontrol, voucher). This rule is about CORRECTION only — never add a global
    zero-invoice refusal.

## D. Where it is enforced
14. **Submit** — under the invoice lock, after flag, actor, pilot branch, identity
    and RME-branch checks, through `prePaymentIneligibility()` (ValidationException
    on `rme_invoice_id`; nothing written, no audit row — like every eligibility
    refusal).
15. **Approve (= apply, D1a)** — after the staleness check proved the invoice is
    still the reviewed document, BEFORE the SUBMITTED→APPROVED write: refused with
    nothing written (not APPROVED, not APPLIED). A changed invoice is marked STALE
    exactly as before (staleness first — a STALE correction can never apply).
16. **Recovery apply** (pre-D1a APPROVED rows) — same position and effect; the row
    stays APPROVED and cancellable — by its MAKER only, and only while that maker is
    an ACTIVE account (§25). APPROVED cannot move to REJECTED, so a pre-D1a APPROVED
    Rp0-original row whose maker was deactivated has no release path and holds its
    invoice behind D10. Production held 0 corrections at this release, so the case
    is theoretical; if it ever exists, the recovery is an owner-approved, audited
    reactivation of the maker → cancel → deactivation — never a data edit.
17. **Reject and cancel are never gated** — they only de-escalate and are the D10
    release path for a pre-existing zero-original correction.
18. The cashier panel shows the refusal text instead of the request button; the
    request form shows the not-eligible notice. Both are courtesy; the service is
    the control.

## E. Historical data immutability
19. No historical invoice, item, payment, receivable, snapshot, document version,
    correction or audit row is changed by this rule. Invoice #36 stays Rp0 UNPAID;
    #41 stays PARTIAL. No migration, no backfill, no data repair. Any repair of an
    existing zero-original correction row needs its own owner approval.

## F. Preserved, unchanged (regression-verified)
20. Positive originals: a valid pre-payment correction still submits, approves
    (= applies v0/v1 atomically) and audits; a later version of an originally
    POSITIVE invoice still corrects.
21. Manual pricing D21 (`CORRECTION_MANUAL_ENTRY` provenance, no tariff gate).
22. Maker-checker D1; automatic apply D1a; SPN4 pilot D1c; identity D1e (29 submits,
    1 approves/rejects/applies); immutable snapshots D7; payment blocking D10;
    invoice-version binding; explicit actors (rules 197/198/199); OD-1 / OD-1a.
23. Existing refusals: paid, partially paid, negative total, new Rp0 line/total,
    stale version/content, unauthorized branch/identity, self-approval, duplicate
    active correction, invalid state, flag disabled.

## G. Explicit actor and active account (rule 200 at the correction service)
24. `assertActor()` re-reads the account through `ActiveAccountResolver` (the ONE
    usable-account definition) and asks the permission and the branch scope of THAT
    persisted account — never of roles a stale in-memory object carries.
25. An inactive, soft-deleted or unsaved account is refused with `ACTOR_UNUSABLE`
    on EVERY mutation — submit, approve, reject, cancel, apply. Cancel was not
    identity-gated, so before this rule an inactive maker could cancel through a
    direct service call (over HTTP `EnsureAccountIsActive` already stopped it).
26. The identity allowlist keeps its own usable check (UI courtesy, readiness
    probe, second barrier) — proven separately in the identity suite.

## H. Concurrency (PostgreSQL, real services in a child process)
27. Lock order unchanged: INVOICE row FOR UPDATE, then the correction row. The
    guard reads only under that lock; no new lock, no lock on `users`.
28. **Rejection vs payment** (`RmeInvoiceCorrectionRejectionDeactivationRaceTest`
    A1/A2): a payment waits on a mid-flight REAL rejection; if it commits, the
    payment is accepted (version unchanged, exactly one payment); if it rolls back,
    D10 refuses the payment and records nothing; a later rejection releases it.
29. **Deactivation vs mutation** (B1/B2). LINEARIZATION POINT = the account re-read
    in `assertActor()`, taken after the invoice lock is held:
    - deactivation committed before it (even while the mutation waited on the lock)
      → refused `ACTOR_UNUSABLE`, nothing written, attempt audited after rollback;
    - deactivation not yet committed at it → the mutation proceeds and is ordered
      BEFORE the deactivation, even if the deactivation commits while the mutation's
      transaction is still open. Serializable (mutation, then deactivation): the
      mutation never writes the account row. A late deactivation never retroactively
      undoes an authorized commit; it stops the NEXT mutation.
    - Serialization order is NOT commit order: an observer can see "account
      inactive" and only later see the correction APPLIED by that account, with
      `decided_at` / audit ids after the deactivation. An emergency deactivation is
      therefore not instantaneous for a mutation already past its authorization
      read; to stop corrections at once use the flag (rule 195 rollback).
    Do not add a `FOR SHARE`/`FOR UPDATE` on `users` to "strengthen" this without a
    new lock-order analysis — it would make every deactivation and login wait on
    corrections and is not required by the invariant.
30. The race suites skip (stated) off PostgreSQL; a skip is never a PASS.

## H2. Residuals (recorded, not closed by this rule)
30a. Line-level Rp0 → positive on a POSITIVE invoice: the builder refuses only a
     non-KEEP line whose RESULT is Rp0, so a free (Rp0) consultation/Kontrol line on
     a positive invoice can still be repriced upward. That is the D21g policy
     question itself; this rule is total-based by the owner's instruction and does
     not invent a line-level pricing policy.
30b. The guard sits in `prePaymentIneligibility()` (PRE_PAYMENT submit). Approve and
     apply are type-agnostic. A future correction type (post-payment) must call the
     guard explicitly at its own submit.
30c. `replayFor()` runs before `assertActor()` in submit: an inactive maker can
     re-read their OWN existing correction through an idempotent replay. Read-only;
     pre-existing.

## I. Audit
31. Authorization refusals (`ACTOR_UNUSABLE`, identity, permission, branch) write
    `RME_INVOICE_CORRECTION_REFUSED` after the rollback. The zero-original refusal is
    an eligibility refusal: rolled back, no audit row, no event, no snapshot, no
    version increment. A refused act never leaves a SUBMITTED/APPROVED/APPLIED trace.

## J. Tests and CI
32. `RmeInvoiceCorrectionZeroOriginalGuardTest` (SQLite + PostgreSQL) and
    `RmeInvoiceCorrectionRejectionDeactivationRaceTest` (PostgreSQL) are declared in
    `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites` and selected by the
    `RmeInvoiceCorrection` token in BOTH critical-gate variants. Verify in the CI
    log that both ran (race tests not skipped) — never infer from a green check.
33. Full Suite follows the standing deferral policy: report `SKIPPED`, never PASS.

## K. Release, verification, rollback
34. Deploy only by running `scripts/deploy-vps-runner.sh` INSIDE the VPS; complete
    only at `exit=0` + `DEPLOY OK` + `DEPLOY_HEAD_TARGET_MATCH=YES`.
35. Production verification is read-only: deployed SHA, guard present in the
    deployed file, financial fingerprint unchanged (or every delta classified),
    #36/#41 unchanged, 0 corrections created, flags/permissions unchanged. Never
    create a real correction to "test" the guard.
36. Scoped GO tag `fix-rme-invoice-correction-zero-original-guard-1-go` on the
    deployed runtime SHA only. It is NOT the pilot's full GO
    (`verification-rme-invoice-correction-prepayment-pilot-1-go`, rule 196).
37. Rollback = code-only redeploy of the previous GO tag
    (`hardening-rme-active-user-patient-selection-1-go`); no data restore. If the
    correction capability must stop instead, use the flag (rule 195 rollback) —
    never disable a payment check.
38. Cleanup: task-owned containers, worktrees, processes and temp files only.
39. Full pilot status is unchanged by this rule: still NO_GO until an authorized
    positive real case is corrected (rule 196).

## L. Superseded in part (historical text kept in each rule)
- Rule 196 §23 — "the ban on correcting the Rp0 invoice #36 is PROCEDURAL, not
  refused in code": SUPERSEDED — it is refused in code (§C/§D here). The procedural
  ban stays as a second layer.
- Rule 197 §14 / rule 200 §E — an unusable correction actor is refused by
  `assertActor()` (`ACTOR_UNUSABLE`) before the identity allowlist
  (`PILOT_IDENTITY_ACTOR_UNUSABLE`) is reached.
